-- title:
--   p. 18, proof of Theorem 6 (converse) — every utility in V_i is of the form (46) with nonnegative multipliers
-- statement:
--   Let $y_{i1},\dots,y_{in}$ be real numbers and let $v \in V_i$, that is, $v : \mathbb R \to \mathbb R$ is concave and nondecreasing, piecewise linear with break points only at the $y_{ik}$, and $v(t) = 0$ for all $t \ge \max_k y_{ik}$. Then there exist $\mu_{i1},\dots,\mu_{in} \ge 0$ such that
--   $$v(t) = -\sum_{k=1}^n \mu_{ik} (y_{ik} - t)_+ \quad \text{for all } t \in \mathbb R.$$
--
--   Together with the fact that (46) with $\mu \ge 0$ lies in $V_i$, this makes $V_i$ exactly the set of utilities generated by nonnegative multipliers of the dominance constraints (39), which is what lets the conditions of Theorem 6 be translated back into the standard sufficient optimality conditions.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The paper's construction ($\nu_k = v'_-(t_k) - v'_+(t_k)$ spread evenly over $J(k) = \{j : y_{ij} = t_k\}$) is a proof and is not stated; it needs $t_1 < \dots < t_K$ to list all distinct values $y_{ij}$, with $\nu_k = 0$ allowed. The page writes "$\mu_{ij}$" for what (46) calls $\mu_{ik}$.
-- source:
--   Dentcheva, Ruszczyński, Optimality and duality theory for stochastic optimization problems with nonlinear dominance constraints, author manuscript (rev. April 2003; Math. Program. 2004, DOI 10.1007/s10107-003-0453-z), p. 18, proof of Theorem 6 (converse), with Eq. (46) (p. 17) and the definition of V_i (p. 16)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_NonlinSSD_Discrete_Problem

open Finset

namespace NonlinSSD.Discrete

theorem V_eq_multiplierUtility {n : ℕ} (y : Fin n → ℝ) (v : ℝ → ℝ) (hv : InV y v) :
    ∃ μ : Fin n → ℝ, (∀ k, 0 ≤ μ k) ∧ ∀ t, v t = multiplierUtility y μ t := by sorry

end NonlinSSD.Discrete
